[Music]  hi I'm Mike at Roskam here at cube Con  in Copenhagen and I'm here with Craig  Peters from J frog how are you I'm doing  great how about yourself  great so tell me a little bit about what  G frog does sure so J frog is a  repository manager and platform for  enabling software developers to  collaborate effectively across  distributed teams with all the different  package types they use all the different  tools and do it in a way that safe and  allows software to be delivered faster  to any end point what do you mean by way  that's safe how does that work with  containers oh that's a really great  question  so glad you asked it's as though we  staged or practice this yeah we didn't  so what I mean by safe is that when you  deploy an application into any  environment so let's take kubernetes is  an example why would we be talking about  that here but if you're deploying to  kubernetes you have meant you might have  packaged your application in helm right  so you might have home sharks that  you're using to deploy the application  those depend on docker images right  those docker images you know where they  are they're in some sort of secure  registry how do you know what's in those  containers so what Jake frog artifactory  provides we call it the kubernetes  registry the kubernetes registry of  artifactory allows you to have in one  place the source of truth for all of the  data all of the package types all of the  dependencies that have been built into  the container image and then packaged  into a home targeting used to deploy the  application you can use any other  packaging type it doesn't depend on home  but essentially we act as a docker  registry that understands all the  dependencies so you know where things  are and then as a repository manager  what that means is that you can create  different repos for different teams at  different stages of development  lifecycle so I have a different one for  my app to app app testing you know  exactly exactly dev QA production so  production can only read from production  repositories and then you know you don't  want to duplicate all those things we  essentially have a promotion model so  based on you might want to make it from  one registry to another or something  like that actually automatically and  it's one we like you want to make it so  only service accounts can do that and  then you want to make that completely  independent of the underlying storage  and use any off mechanism right that's  that's what we do and do you want to  talk me through what's happening right  here absolutely so the story here is  that you're deploying an application  that's packaged as docker images inside  a home chart to do kubernetes right well  what's in it it's docker images if you  look at an image it actually is usually  layers all right we use layering and all  that that consists of your application  whatever code you've written maybe it's  a maybe it's maven right you might have  written Java and use a build tool or it  might be MPM and you know you've got  that bit you've got the packages you  dependent upon which are like external  packages you might have operating  systems so the idea from left to right  is that using a common registry  framework you can manage exactly all the  way to the lowest level and that's  actually really important for companies  that where the software production life  pipeline is their production and you  can't be blocked if you have an issue  getting to the public npm for example  right you keep that and that stops  people in reality and so what we do is  we create what's called a virtual  repository that sits on top of your  local repo and the remoted repos that  you have a local cache of and that  allows organizations to have really good  control over what packages go into their  applications so tell me about graph is  and how our sorry tell me how how Jay  Froggers with Professor graffities  Google's open source metadata image  server and you were one of the launch  partners for that back in October how  does that how does that work together  absolutely so one of the things you have  to do in this kind of a scenario is you  have to have a really deep understanding  of the metadata about all of the  different package types graph a is is a  open API for exchanging metadata about  those assets between many different  systems a lot of different tools in the  ecosystem will  different kinds of information about  those so the the first things we started  with obviously our vulnerabilities  everybody wants security and safety so  yeah so one of our products is called  x-ray and x-ray is a part of this  package and what it does is it has a  deep understanding of the layered  packaging it unpacks them all  understands the components that go into  a package matches them against  vulnerabilities but we also do licenses  and things like that the point of graph  fayus is that it makes it very easy and  lightweight to exchange information  between US and other graph AS data  providers so say google has a huge  database of different kinds of des like  maybe performance our compatibility data  you need a way to aggregate that in your  CI pipeline so that you can make  automated decisions about whether or not  something you know your software is  ready to move from staging into  production right and so you don't want  to have to go and query all of the  different systems x-ray provides way  using graph a us to aggregate all that  data into one system one call great well  thank you so much for chatting with us  that the floor at Keep Calm having a  good time here oh it's been great we've  had lots of great conversations and you  know the we're really glad to be here  and participating this community thank  you so much hopefully we'll see you in  Seattle and and thank you all for  joining and watching us here at cube con

